text:
\begin{prop}[Non-triviality of the phase transition]
For every $d\geq 2$, nearest-neighbour Bernoulli bond percolation on $\Z^d$ satisfies $0<p_c(\Z^d)<1$.
\end{prop}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative susceptibility inequality]
Let $G$ be a connected, unimodular, transitive weighted graph normalized so that $|J|=1$. Then for every $0<\beta<\beta_c$,
\[
  \frac{\dd\chi_\beta}{\dd\beta}
  \geq
  \frac{\chi_\beta(\chi_\beta-\nabla_\beta)}
       {3\beta^2\nabla_\beta^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Completion of the proof of the quantitative susceptibility inequality]
Substituting \(B_\beta\leq A_\beta\leq\nabla_\beta^2\) into the intermediate inequality gives the theorem. The important point is that the proof replaces a small-open-triangle surgery argument by a global diagram comparison.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Generalized Dewan--Muirhead entropy comparison]
Let $G=(V,E,J)$ be a countable weighted graph, let $A\subseteq\{0,1\}^E$ be Borel, and let $T$ be a decision tree that Borel-computes $A$. Then
\[
  |\P_{\beta_1}(A)-\P_{\beta_2}(A)|^2
  \leq
  \frac{|\beta_1-\beta_2|^2}{\min\{\beta_1,\beta_2\}}
  \max\{\P_{\beta_1}(A),\P_{\beta_2}(A)\}
  \sum_{e\in E}J_e\operatorname{Rev}_{\beta_1}(T,e).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Gladkov's three-point inequality]
For Bernoulli percolation on any graph,
\[
  \tau_p(x,y,z)
  \leq
  \sqrt{
    8\,\tau_p(x,y)\tau_p(y,z)\tau_p(z,x)
  }.
\]
\end{theorem}

There are therefore several distinct regimes:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item high-dimensional effectively long-range behaviour, where $d>3\alpha$ and $\alpha<2$, so mean-field exponents hold with stable spatial scaling;
\item critical-dimensional effectively long-range behaviour, where $d=3\alpha$ and logarithmic corrections appear;
\item low-dimensional effectively long-range behaviour, where $d<3\alpha$ but long edges still govern macroscopic connectivity;
\item effectively short-range behaviour, where the long-range tail is irrelevant and the model should behave like nearest-neighbour percolation.
\end{itemize}
The boundaries between these regimes are subtle. Sak's prediction locates the crossover between long-range and short-range behaviour using the short-range anomalous dimension.

The same regimes can also be read through the finite-box diagnostic from the hyperscaling chapter. In a box of side length $R$, the schematic large-cluster picture is
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{regime}
&
\#\text{ large clusters in }B_R
&
\text{typical large-cluster volume}
\\ \hline
\mathrm{LR\mbox{-}HD}
&
R^{d-3\alpha}
&
R^{2\alpha}
\\
\mathrm{mSR\mbox{-}HD}
&
R^{d-6}(\log R)^3
&
R^4(\log R)^{-2}
\\
\mathrm{SR\mbox{-}HD}
&
R^{d-6}
&
R^4
\\
\mathrm{LR\mbox{-}CD}
&
\log R
&
R^{2\alpha}(\log R)^{-1/2}
\\
\mathrm{LR\mbox{-}LD}
&
O(1)
&
R^{(d+\alpha)/2}.
\end{array}
\]
This table should be treated as a power-counting guide unless the hypotheses of the corresponding theorems have been stated. Its main purpose here is to show what changes across regimes. In low effective dimension the order-one large-cluster picture is restored, while in high effective dimension the number of large clusters grows polynomially. At the critical dimension that polynomial growth is replaced by a logarithm.

The compact output, in the regimes where the required two-point and localization hypotheses have been verified, is as follows. In all three effectively long-range regimes
\[
  \E_{\beta_c,r}|K|\asymp r^\alpha,
\]
while
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{regime}
&
\E_{\beta_c,r}|K|^2
&
\P_{\beta_c}(|K|\geq n)
\\ \hline
\mathrm{HD}
&
r^{3\alpha}
&
n^{-1/2}
\\
\mathrm{LR\mbox{-}CD}
&
r^{3\alpha}/\sqrt{\log r}
&
(\log n)^{1/4}n^{-1/2}
\\
\mathrm{LR\mbox{-}LD}
&
r^{(d+3\alpha)/2}
&
n^{-(d-\alpha)/(d+\alpha)} .
\end{array}
\]
Here $\mathrm{HD}$ includes the high-dimensional long-range case $d>3\alpha$ and the corresponding short-range high-dimensional cases covered by the lace-expansion input, $\mathrm{LR\mbox{-}CD}$ denotes the long-range critical dimension $d=3\alpha<6$, and $\mathrm{LR\mbox{-}LD}$ denotes the low-effective-dimensional effectively-long-range regime. This table is a guide to the proof rather than a replacement for its hypotheses: the high-dimensional entries come from closed moment ODEs, the critical-dimensional entry from the same ODEs with a marginal logarithmic error, and the low-dimensional entry from the hydrodynamic and hyperscaling analysis later in the chapter.

Writing
\[
  \xi_2(r)^2=
  \frac{
    \mathbb E_{\beta_c,r}\bigl[\sum_{x\in K}\norm{x}^2\bigr]
  }{
    \mathbb E_{\beta_c,r}|K|
  },
\]
one obtains
\[
  \xi_2(r)\asymp
  \begin{cases}
    r, & \alpha<2,\\
    r\sqrt{\log r}, & \alpha=2,\\
    r^{\alpha/2}, & \alpha>2.
  \end{cases}
\]
Thus the same high-dimensional volume-tail theorem can lead to different spatial scaling limits: super-Levy behaviour in the genuinely long-range regime, super-Brownian behaviour in the effectively short-range regime, and logarithmic interpolation at $\alpha=2$.

The first dependency chain is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\varphi_\beta(S)\text{ and ghost fields}
&\Longrightarrow \text{finite susceptibility below }\beta_c\\
&\Longrightarrow \text{subcritical exponential tails}\\
&\Longrightarrow \text{legitimate finite-moment input for later arguments}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The ghost field is important because it repairs the otherwise circular step of dividing by a possibly infinite susceptibility. This small technical point recurs throughout the book: one often proves a statement first for a regularized observable, then removes the regularization by monotone convergence or Tauberian theory.

The third layer asks what the clusters actually look like. In high dimensions, the answer is governed by branching random walk and superprocess limits. In two dimensions, RSW gluing, arm exponents, and Gladkov-type inequalities force a Mobius-covariant $k$-point geometry. In long-range models, the proof path is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{two-point control}
&\Longrightarrow \text{cutoff moment ODEs}\\
&\Longrightarrow \text{size-biased limits and ghost fields}\\
&\Longrightarrow \text{volume tails and scaling limits}.
\end{aligned}
\]
